  "authoredNote": "line_number values transcribed from a measured run and checked against the numbered source: declarations on 14-16, `build` on 19 with its recipe on 20, `info` on 23 with its recipe on 24, `foreach-binder` on 28 with its recipe on 29. The `$(DESTDIR)usr/bin/app` prerequisite is recorded under its own text AND its inner reference is recorded separately — the dependency is on the expansion, the reference is on the variable, and they are different facts.",
  "rationale": "The BUILTIN / EXTERNAL / PROJECT split, which decides the relationship LABEL. A Make prerequisite is usually a FILE and a Make variable is often set by the command line, the environment, or another makefile, and GNU Make ships builtins no file can declare. Publishing any of those as a project-internal `references` asserts a declaration that does not exist: over the corpus, 231 unresolved edge ends were file paths, 187 external variables and 160 builtins. So `references` is emitted only when THIS file declares the target, and `uses` carries the rest — labelled, so the class is visible rather than pooled. Line 20 deliberately reads a declared variable and an undeclared one on the SAME recipe line, so the fixture fails if the two classes are ever collapsed back together.",
